+# Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope; Filtering the Directory by a Displayed Specialty Is In Scope
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian, on the reasoning that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once and repeating it elsewhere adds surface rather than understanding. The row read as a single boundary, but it covered two different capabilities: typing free text to find a veterinarian, and narrowing the already-published directory to an attribute it already shows. The first repeats owner search; the second does not — it filters a fixed, displayed collection by an exact value.
+
+A request to filter the veterinarian directory by specialty forced the distinction. Read against the undivided NG-9, the request looked out of scope. The owner decided the boundary (2026-08-26).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Keep NG-9 whole and decline the filter.** Rejected: it conflates free-text search with attribute filtering. The filter narrows a collection the directory already presents by a value it already shows; it does not add a search surface.
+2. **Withdraw NG-9 entirely.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian search still adds surface without teaching a new pattern, and there is no reason to open it.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen). Free-text search for pets, visits, and veterinarians stays out; filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays comes in.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian remains out of scope. Filtering the published veterinarian directory by a specialty it already shows is in scope and is realized by `REQ-VET-003`. The filter applies to both the HTML page and the machine-readable form of the directory, matches a whole specialty name without regard to letter case, and is requested through the address alone — no on-page control in this round.
+
+The same decision reinstates the machine-readable directory endpoint, previously withdrawn as an unrequested artifact (`REQ-VET-002`), as a supported surface. The withdrawn id is retired and not reused.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open-provenance consultation as a narrowed, decided row; the reason on the row still holds for the free-text search it continues to decline.
+- The veterinarian directory gains its first requested filtering capability on both surfaces under `REQ-VET-003`.
+- A visible filter control is not part of this decision; it may follow as a separate request.
+- Two questions stay open and are recorded in the PRD: whether the machine-readable form paginates a narrowed result, and how a specialty named more than once in one request is treated. Both take the narrowest reading until a consumer forces them.
+- Narrowing this row further, or opening free-text veterinarian search, is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR, per the Non-Goals table convention.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the in-scope filtering capability.
